// ============================================================
// 🔥 CONCURRENCY ANOMALIES: WRITE SKEW & PHANTOM WRITE
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// 🧠 0. PEHLE FOUNDATION SAMAJH (VERY IMPORTANT)
// ============================================================

// Problem kya hai?
// DB me multiple transactions ek saath run karte hain (concurrent execution)

// Har transaction:
// 1. Data READ karta hai
// 2. Decision leta hai
// 3. Data WRITE karta hai

// Issue:
// Agar 2 transactions same data dekh ke decision lete hain,
// toh final result galat ho sakta hai

// 👉 Isko bolte hain "Concurrency Anomaly"


// ============================================================
// 🔥 1. WRITE SKEW (CORE CONCEPT)
// ============================================================

// 🧠 INTUITION (REAL LIFE):
// Fridge me 2 pizza hai 🍕🍕
// Rule: At least 1 pizza hamesha rehna chahiye

// Person A: "Pizza hai?" → YES → kha liya
// Person B: "Pizza hai?" → YES → kha liya

// Final: 0 pizza ❌ rule break

// 👉 Yehi WRITE SKEW hai


// ============================================================
// 💡 DEFINITION:
// ============================================================

// Write Skew tab hota hai jab:
// - 2 transactions SAME condition read karte hain
// - Lekin DIFFERENT rows update karte hain
// - Aur milke constraint tod dete hain


// ============================================================
// 🧪 CLASSIC EXAMPLE (INTERVIEW FAVORITE)
// ============================================================

/*
Table: Doctors(id, on_call)

Rule:
At least 1 doctor must be on call
*/

// Initial State:
// A = ON
// B = ON


// ----------------------
// Transaction T1
// ----------------------
// Step 1: Check B → ON (safe)
// Step 2: Set A = OFF


// ----------------------
// Transaction T2
// ----------------------
// Step 1: Check A → ON (safe)
// Step 2: Set B = OFF


// ----------------------
// FINAL STATE
// ----------------------
// A = OFF
// B = OFF ❌ (constraint violated)


// ============================================================
// ❌ WHY WRITE SKEW HAPPENED?
// ============================================================

// 🔥 MOST IMPORTANT LINE:
// DB locks ROWS, not BUSINESS LOGIC

// T1 ne A ko lock kiya
// T2 ne B ko lock kiya

// 👉 No conflict → DB allowed both

// BUT rule depend karta tha:
// A + B (combined state)

// 👉 DB ko ye pata hi nahi tha ki ye dangerous hai


// ============================================================
// 🛠️ WRITE SKEW SOLUTIONS
// ============================================================

// ----------------------
// ✅ Solution 1: SERIALIZABLE
// ----------------------

// DB force karta hai sequential execution

// T1 pura run karega → phir T2

// T1: A = OFF
// T2: A ko check karega → OFF → STOP

// ✔ Safe but thoda slow


// ----------------------
// ✅ Solution 2: LOCK ALL RELATED ROWS
// ----------------------

// Instead of locking only A or B,
// dono rows ko lock karo

/*
SQL:
SELECT * FROM Doctors WHERE on_call = true FOR UPDATE;
*/

// 👉 T1 aur T2 dono same rows lock karne ki koshish karenge
// 👉 Ek wait karega

// ✔ Conflict visible → problem solved


// ----------------------
// ✅ Solution 3: MATERIALIZE CONSTRAINT
// ----------------------

// 🧠 Idea:
// Constraint ko ek SINGLE row me convert karo

// Example:

/*
Table: OnCallCount(count = 2)
*/

// T1: count-- → 1
// T2: count-- → 0 ❌ not allowed

// 👉 Dono same row update kar rahe hain
// 👉 DB easily conflict detect karega

// ✔ Very powerful technique


// ============================================================
// 🔥 2. PHANTOM WRITE (ADVANCED VERSION)
// ============================================================

// 🧠 INTUITION:

// Write Skew me:
// Rows EXIST karte the

// Phantom me:
// Rows EXIST hi nahi karte initially


// ============================================================
// 💡 DEFINITION:
// ============================================================

// Phantom Write tab hota hai jab:
// - Transactions condition check karte hain
// - Rows exist nahi karte
// - Dono insert kar dete hain
// - Final state me conflict aa jata hai


// ============================================================
// 🧪 EXAMPLE (SUPER IMPORTANT)
// ============================================================

/*
Table: Meetings(room_id, time)

Rule:
Ek room me ek hi time pe sirf 1 meeting ho sakti hai
*/


// Initial:
// No meeting at (Room1, 10AM)


// ----------------------
// Transaction T1
// ----------------------
// Check: koi meeting nahi → OK
// Insert: (Room1, 10AM)


// ----------------------
// Transaction T2
// ----------------------
// Check: koi meeting nahi → OK
// Insert: (Room1, 10AM)


// ----------------------
// FINAL
// ----------------------
// 2 meetings at same time ❌


// ============================================================
// ❌ WHY PHANTOM WRITE HAPPENED?
// ============================================================

// 🔥 GOLDEN LINE:
// DB cannot lock what DOES NOT EXIST

// Jab T1 ne check kiya → row nahi thi
// Jab T2 ne check kiya → row nahi thi

// 👉 Isliye koi lock nahi laga

// 👉 Dono ne insert kar diya


// ============================================================
// 🛠️ PHANTOM WRITE SOLUTION
// ============================================================

// ❌ Direct locking kaam nahi karega
// kyunki row exist hi nahi karti


// ----------------------
// ✅ SOLUTION: MATERIALIZE CONFLICT
// ----------------------

// 🧠 Idea:
// Rows pehle se bana do (pre-create)

// Example:

/*
Table: Slots(room, time, booked)

Pre-created rows:
(Room1, 10AM, false)
*/


// ----------------------
// Transaction Flow
// ----------------------

/*
SELECT * FROM Slots
WHERE room=1 AND time=10AM
FOR UPDATE;
*/

// Then:
/// booked = true


// ----------------------
// KYA HOGA?
// ----------------------

// T1 aur T2 dono same row lock karne aayenge
// Ek wait karega

// 👉 Only one booking possible

// ✔ Problem solved


// ============================================================
// 🔥 WRITE SKEW vs PHANTOM WRITE
// ============================================================

// Write Skew:
// - Existing rows pe kaam
// - UPDATE operation
// - Multi-row constraint break

// Phantom Write:
// - Non-existing rows
// - INSERT operation
// - Conflict invisible at read time


// ============================================================
// 🧠 CORE INSIGHT (INTERVIEW GOLD)
// ============================================================

// 👉 "If DB cannot SEE the conflict, it cannot PREVENT it"

// Write Skew:
// Conflict hidden across multiple rows

// Phantom:
// Conflict hidden because row not present

// 👉 Solution always:
// Make conflict VISIBLE to DB


// ============================================================
// 🔥 INTERVIEW READY ANSWER
// ============================================================

// Q: Why Repeatable Read fails?

// Ans:
// "Because it ensures row-level consistency,
// but fails to prevent predicate-based anomalies
// like write skew and phantom inserts."


// ============================================================
// 🚀 FINAL REVISION SHORT NOTES
// ============================================================

// Write Skew:
// Read same data → update different rows → break constraint

// Phantom:
// Check passes → insert happens → later conflict

// Fix:
// - Serializable isolation
// - Explicit locking
// - Materializing conflicts

// ============================================================